<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; text-align: center; padding: 15px 0; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Changing Forests – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Changing Forests</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Our Ecology Is Changing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow the linear structure of a short formal talk. Interactive: Respond to questions and arguments in debates.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow the linear sequence of points on a short history of local ecology (e.g., "You can see from the photo in 2020 there was more rainforest in the area than in 2025.")
            Can respond to questions in arguments or debates on forest change (e.g., "The author makes a good argument about cutting down the forest for farming. It has a big impact on the environment.")
            Can identify and discuss key points in a structured talk on ecological issues (e.g., "The first part of the talk was about the water cycle of rainforests.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of key concepts and target language related to "How Our Ecology Is Changing".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a sequence of four images on the board, showing a local forest area changing over time (e.g., "Forest in 2000," "Forest in 2010 with some logging," "Forest in 2020 with less trees," "Forest in 2024 with significant deforestation and construction"). Students briefly discuss in pairs what they observe, focusing on the changes. The teacher asks, "What has changed in these pictures over the years?" (Present perfect trigger).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces 8-10 key vocabulary words visually and orally, writing them on the board: <i>deforestation, biodiversity, forest, conservation, ecosystems, species, affect, protect, significant, environmental</i>. The teacher models their pronunciation, specifically focusing on the /aɪ/ sound in "biodiversity," "life," and "type," and the reduction of -ate in "climate," "approximate," and "accurate" (if applicable from other words). Word stress in "deforestation," "ecological," and "environmental" is also emphasized. For instance, "Deforestation has significantly affected the local biodiversity. If we protect our forests, we will maintain healthier ecosystems."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is 'deforestation'? Is it planting trees or cutting them down?" (Expected answer: Cutting them down.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a forest has 'biodiversity', does it have many different plants and animals, or just a few?" (Expected answer: Many different plants and animals.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content through an authentic text, dialogue, or audio/visual material connected to the scenario "Our Changing Forests."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A 2-3 minute audio clip from a formal talk or a short documentary on "The Impact of Deforestation on Local Biodiversity in Central America." The register is informative and semi-formal.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student comprehends and analyzes oral texts of varying complexity in different contexts and for different purposes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Pre-listening Task:</b> Students read a short list of terms they will hear (e.g., <i>local ecosystems, native species, significant impact, conservation efforts</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>First Listening (Gist):</b> Students listen to the audio once and answer one or two general comprehension questions:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the main topic of the talk?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What has been the most significant change mentioned in the talk?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Second Listening (Details):</b> Students listen again and complete True/False statements or a multiple-choice activity related to specific details (e.g., "Deforestation has increased dramatically since 2010. True/False," "The talk suggests planting more trees will improve air quality. True/False").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy-focused activities for listening comprehension, grammar, and vocabulary related to the theme.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific information; using Present Perfect, First Conditional, Comparatives; target vocabulary (<i>deforestation, biodiversity, ecosystems, conservation, affect, protect, significant</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listening Gap-Fill (Controlled Practice):</b> Students listen to short sentences extracted from the audio (or similar sentences) with gaps for the target vocabulary or grammar. Example: "Deforestation ______ significantly ______ local ecosystems over the past decade." (has, affected). The teacher plays each sentence twice, pausing for students to fill in the blanks.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Sentence Completion (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher plays the beginning of a conditional sentence related to the audio, and students complete it orally or in writing, using their own ideas based on what they heard. Example: "If we continue to cut down forests..." (students complete: "...we will lose many species" or "...biodiversity will decrease"). Another example: "Urban areas have ______ biodiversity compared to natural forests." (less).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Discussion and Peer Check:</b> Students work in pairs to compare their answers from the previous activities. They discuss why they chose certain words or completed sentences in a particular way, using phrases like, "I think 'deforestation' is correct because the speaker mentioned cutting down trees." or "If we protect our forests, we will help to reduce climate change." This allows for polite expression of environmental concerns.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students use the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Changing Forests." Focus on fluency and collaborative interaction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Mini-Debate / Structured Discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Group Formation:</b> Students are divided into small groups of 3-4.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Scenario Presentation:</b> The teacher presents a prompt related to the audio and the theme: "Imagine your local community is debating whether to cut down a section of a nearby forest for a new agricultural project. Some people argue it will bring jobs and economic growth; others worry about the environmental impact."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Role Assignment:</b> Within each group, students briefly decide on roles (e.g., "Pro-Development," "Pro-Conservation," "Community Moderator").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>4. Discussion:</b> Students engage in a structured discussion/mini-debate for about 5-7 minutes. They must use information from the listening text and the target vocabulary/grammar to express their arguments. They are encouraged to use phrases for asking about environmental impact ("How has deforestation affected our area?") and discussing conservation ideas ("If we plant more trees, we can improve air quality."). They should also practice expressing concerns politely ("Protecting our forests benefits everyone.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>5. Reporting:</b> Each group briefly reports one key argument from each side to the class. Example: "One argument for cutting down the forest is job creation, but if we lose the forest, biodiversity will dramatically decrease."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategies, evaluation criteria, and student self-assessment for the lesson's learning outcomes.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the Performance stage.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' ability to actively participate in the group discussion, respond to arguments, and articulate key points from the listening text and their own ideas.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies key points from the formal talk.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds to questions/arguments effectively.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., deforestation, biodiversity, conservation) appropriately.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Applies Present Perfect and Conditional structures correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation is intelligible (especially /aɪ/ and word stress).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engages collaboratively and expresses concerns politely.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts individually:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new thing I learned about forest change today is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found it easy/difficult to understand the main points of the talk because..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review of the lesson and connection to the next lesson in the sequence.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel using new vocabulary like 'deforestation' and 'biodiversity' when talking about environmental issues?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of the listening activity helped me the most to understand the topic?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The listening activities were effective for introducing key vocabulary and grammar. Students engaged well with the debate scenario, showing good comprehension of the linear structure of the talk and making efforts to respond to arguments. Some students struggled with the accurate use of second conditional sentences, requiring more scaffolding next time. The pronunciation focus on /aɪ/ was successful, but explicit practice for -ate word endings could be added to future warm-ups.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2, students will encounter a written text on a similar ecological topic, using the same core vocabulary introduced today. This will reinforce their understanding and provide another context for the target language, preparing them for reading comprehension tasks.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>